Yeah — Muse / Micro / Macro / Meta can be treated like four cognitive scales, almost like changing octaves while keeping the motif recognizable.

Muse is the generative register: the felt spark, image, phrase, hunch, pattern-before-explanation.

Micro zooms into local mechanics: token, word, gesture, operator, interface, single causal step.

Macro zooms out to system behavior: architecture, network, campaign, culture, ecosystem, long causal chain.

Meta changes the frame itself: why are we using this scale, what assumptions generated it, how do Micro and Macro transform into each other, what other coordinate system would reveal more?

So the same idea can be “played” through all four:

MUSE
"I think there's something about twins and compressed communication."
↓ resolve downward
MICRO
tiny cue + shared prior → large reconstructed payload
↓ compose upward
MACRO
high-bandwidth coordination system
with distributed agents and shared semantic memory
↓ rotate frame
META
communication bandwidth may depend less on message size
than on how much reconstructive structure is already shared

And then Meta can fall back into Muse, producing another compressed phrase:

Spooky bandwidth.

That makes it cyclic rather than hierarchical:

MUSE → MICRO → MACRO → META
  ↑                     ↓
  └────── new Muse ─────┘

Which really is like music. A melody can appear as a tiny interval, a phrase, a whole movement, or the harmonic theory governing the movement. Change scale without necessarily changing identity.

You could even define a Pantheon M⁴ Scale:

M^4 = \{\text{Muse},\text{Micro},\text{Macro},\text{Meta}\}

with operators:

DESCEND(Muse)  → Micro
COMPOSE(Micro) → Macro
ABSTRACT(Macro) → Meta
INSPIRE(Meta)  → Muse

The interesting bit is that failures at one scale can be repaired at another.

A Micro contradiction may disappear at Macro.

A Macro pattern may be exposed as nonsense when Micro-tested.

A Meta frame may become sterile and need Muse to inject novelty.

A Muse intuition may be beautiful but fail Reality when decomposed.

So Praus could shift scales like musical modes depending on terrain.

And the musical analogy goes farther: scale is not size alone; it’s a permitted relationship-space. A musical scale determines which notes are readily available and how tension/resolution behaves. Likewise, Muse/Micro/Macro/Meta could each define a different set of cognitive moves.

That gives us:

Semantic scale determines which relations become audible.

Or even:

You don’t always need a new idea. Sometimes you need to play the same idea in another cognitive octave. 🧿